##############################################################################

entry:joslyn-1995-system
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Joslyn (1995), Semantic Control Systems, §2.6, Definition 21 (System), p. 99"@en ;
    atlas:statedIn bearer:joslyn-1995-semantic-control ;
    atlas:sourceLocation """§2.6 The Synthetic View, Definition 21, p. 99 (page-image transcription section)""" ;
    atlas:verbatim """Thus we arrive at a synthetic definition of system in terms of distinctions, variety, and constraint.

Definition 21 (System) A system is a cardinal distinction on a variety of dimensional distinctions.""" ;
    atlas:displayForm """A system is a cardinal distinction on a variety of dimensional distinctions.""" ;
    atlas:displayContext """Thus we arrive at a synthetic definition of system in terms of distinctions, variety, and constraint.""" ;
    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:cardinal-distinction ,
                           prim:dimensional-distinction ,
                           prim:variety ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDHC ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py; verbatim re-transcribed from the page image by claude-fable-5-1 2026-09-14 ; verbatim read against p. 99 (PDF page 13) by Shingai Thornton 2026-09-14: Def 21 sentence, the ₁ subscripts on system and constraint, S′ and the angle brackets, and \"generated contingently on S\" all confirmed" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """WHY THIS IS THE SYNTHETIC ENTRY. The paper offers three definitions of the author's own: Definition 4 (p. 89, system_J, "my own natural language definition": an entity resulting from parts entering into relation, at a hierarchically distinct level — entry 014), Definition 9 (p. 91, system₂: a bounded region of a space functionally and uniquely distinguished — entry 015), and Definition 21, which he introduces as the point he "arrives at": "a synthetic definition of system in terms of distinctions, variety, and constraint." §2.6 says both kinds of distinction "are clearly required for systems formation": repeated dimensional distinction-making builds "a complex, multi-dimensional universe of discourse (set of qualitatively distinct parts) on which cardinal distinctions can then be made". So 21 = a system₂-type act (a cardinal distinction, a boundary) made on a system_J-type universe (qualitatively distinct parts). Three entries, one author, as with Bunge's two; ruled by Shingai Thornton 2026-09-14. The paper's other labelled definitions (1 Webster, 2 Hall & Fagan, 3 Waelchli, 5 Mesarović = system₁, 8 Gaines, 10 Abbot & van Ness) are restatements of others and are not entries.

PARTS, NOT ENTRIES. Definitions 19 (cardinal distinction: "a constraint₂ which creates a system₁", p. 95) and 20 (dimensional distinction, p. 95) define the definition's two primitives and enter the scheme with those sentences.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED. No cco is-about. CASES: none at this locus."""@en .

entry:joslyn-1995-contingent-metasystem
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Joslyn (1995), Semantic Control Systems, §2.7.1, Definition 22 (Contingent Metasystem), p. 99"@en ;
    atlas:statedIn bearer:joslyn-1995-semantic-control ;
    atlas:sourceLocation """§2.7.1 Contingent Metasystems, Definition 22, p. 99 (page-image transcription section)""" ;
    atlas:verbatim """Definition 22 (Contingent Metasystem) Given a system₁ S with environment E (where E is understood to be similar to the constraint₁ C), then let
S′ := ⟨S, E⟩
be the metasystem, with parts S and E, generated contingently on S.

This point is driven home particularly in the case of simple distinctions, but it also holds in general. In the creation of any system₁ S, another metasystem S′ is identifiable contingent on considering S and its environment together as new, distinct entities.""" ;
    atlas:displayForm """Given a system₁ S with environment E (where E is understood to be similar to the constraint₁ C), then let
S′ := ⟨S, E⟩
be the metasystem, with parts S and E, generated contingently on S.""" ;
    atlas:displayContext """In the creation of any system₁ S, another metasystem S′ is identifiable contingent on considering S and its environment together as new, distinct entities.""" ;
    atlas:authorCaveat """(where E is understood to be similar to the constraint₁ C)""" ;
    atlas:speciesOf entry:mesarovic-takahara-1975-general-system ;
    atlas:kindRestriction "metasystem: the pair ⟨S, E⟩ of a system₁ and its environment, generated contingently on S; genus word in the passage: system₁ = Definition 5 (p. 89), which the author adopts from the Mesarović school (graded a restatement of entry 008), so the genus pointer is Mesarović's Definition 1.1" ;
    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:system-1 ,
                           prim:environment ,
                           prim:constraint-1 ,
                           prim:part ,
                           prim:generated-contingently-on ,
                           prim:metasystem ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDHC ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py; verbatim re-transcribed from the page image by claude-fable-5-1 2026-09-14 ; verbatim read against p. 99 (PDF page 13) by Shingai Thornton 2026-09-14: Def 21 sentence, the ₁ subscripts on system and constraint, S′ and the angle brackets, and \"generated contingently on S\" all confirmed" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """WHY IT IS AN ENTRY AND A SPECIES. A metasystem is a system (it has parts S and E) of a stated kind: formed from a system and its environment. It is the form Definition 25 later takes as given ("Given a metasystem CS := ⟨C, O⟩"), so it is the genus of the control entries and a species of Definition 21. The species chain the catalogue records is 009 (control₂) → 013 (metasystem) → 012 (system).

GENUS WORD (corrected 2026-09-14 against the p. 89 page image). The passage says system₁. In this paper system₁ is Definition 5 (p. 89): "a system₁ S is any relation in X, so that S ⊆ X, S ≠ ∅", introduced with "the Mesarovic school ... from which the following definition is adopted" — a restatement of Mesarović & Takahara 1975 Def 1.1, entry 008 (scan candidate 4, graded PROP). The pointer therefore goes to 008, in the author's own words; it is not a lineage claim by the catalogue. The author's OWN definitions are Definition 4 (system_J, entry 014), Definition 9 (system₂, entry 015) and the synthetic Definition 21 (entry 012); none of them is the genus word of this passage.

PRIMITIVES. environment, constraint-1 and part are reused (same words as existing entries); system-1 and metasystem are new. DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED. No cco is-about. CASES: none at this locus."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Entries 014 and 015 — the author's two senses: system_J (Definition 4) and
# system₂ (Definition 9). Accessioned 2026-09-14 at MDU on Shingai Thornton's
# ruling; verbatims from the page images (pp. 89, 91), the pipeline's OCR
# verbatims having carried "System.)", "5 is", "resultsfromthe", "¿5".
##############################################################################

entry:joslyn-1995-system-j
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Joslyn (1995), Semantic Control Systems, §2.1.1, Definition 4 (System_J), p. 89"@en ;
    atlas:statedIn bearer:joslyn-1995-semantic-control ;
    atlas:sourceLocation """§2.1.1 The Classical or Structuralist View, Definition 4, p. 89 (page-image transcription section)""" ;
    atlas:verbatim """To these I will add my own natural language definition:

Definition 4 (System_J) A system_J S is an entity which results from the entering into relation of multiple entities, called parts, to form the new whole entity S with new properties at a level hierarchically distinct from those parts.""" ;
    atlas:displayForm """A system_J S is an entity which results from the entering into relation of multiple entities, called parts, to form the new whole entity S with new properties at a level hierarchically distinct from those parts.""" ;
    atlas:displayContext """To these I will add my own natural language definition:""" ;
    atlas:authorCaveat """To these I will add my own natural language definition:""" ;
    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:entity ,
                           prim:relation ,
                           prim:part ,
                           prim:whole ,
                           prim:property ,
                           prim:level ;
    atlas:admits case:joslyn-machines-and-organisms ,
                 case:joslyn-lever ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDU ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py; verbatim re-transcribed from the p. 89 page image by claude-fable-5-1 2026-09-14 — not yet human-read" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """SCOPE. The author's own natural-language definition, subscript J, offered after Webster (Def. 1), Hall & Fagan (Def. 2) and Waelchli (Def. 3) and before the Mesarović-school system₁ (Def. 5) he adopts. Admitted on the Bertalanffy-1972 precedent (entry 004): prose, but sorted (parts, whole entity) with a fixed constitutive relation (entering into relation) and a condition (new properties at a hierarchically distinct level). The caveat is the author's own word "natural language".

CASES. Two admitted, from the paragraph that follows: "machines and organisms" as classical exemplars, and the lever (a board and a rock so positioned that the rock is a fulcrum), with the author's own reason — the lifting force "does not follow from considering the rock and board simply together as a collection." The collection-without-arrangement is NOT encoded as a refusal: he does not say it is not a system, only that the lever's properties do not follow from it.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED. No cco is-about. Primitives are lexical; "relation" and "part" reuse existing slugs because the words are the same."""@en .

case:joslyn-machines-and-organisms
    a atlas:Example ;
    rdfs:label "machines and organisms (Joslyn 1995, classical exemplars)"@en ;
    atlas:verbatim "Classical exemplars of systems are machines and organisms." ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "§2.1.1, p. 89, paragraph following Definition 4" ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDU ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-fable-5-1 from the page image, 2026-09-14" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date .

case:joslyn-lever
    a atlas:Example ;
    rdfs:label "the lever: a board and a rock positioned so the rock is a fulcrum (Joslyn 1995)"@en ;
    atlas:verbatim "But when they are so positioned that the rock acts as a fulcrum to the board, then together they form a greater whole machine, a lever, which has properties at a hierarchically distinct level, here lifting force." ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "§2.1.1, p. 89, paragraph following Definition 4" ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDU ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-fable-5-1 from the page image, 2026-09-14" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date .

entry:joslyn-1995-system-2
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Joslyn (1995), Semantic Control Systems, §2.1.2, Definition 9 (System₂), p. 91"@en ;
    atlas:statedIn bearer:joslyn-1995-semantic-control ;
    atlas:sourceLocation """§2.1.2 The Constructivist or Functionalist View, Definition 9, p. 91 (page-image transcription section)""" ;
    atlas:verbatim """I offer the following definition.

Definition 9 (System₂) A system₂ is a bounded region of some (perhaps abstract) space which functionally and uniquely distinguishes it.""" ;
    atlas:displayForm """A system₂ is a bounded region of some (perhaps abstract) space which functionally and uniquely distinguishes it.""" ;
    atlas:displayContext """I offer the following definition.""" ;
    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:region ,
                           prim:space ,
                           prim:bounded ,
                           prim:distinguishes ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:MDU ;
    atlas:encodedBy "claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py; verbatim re-transcribed from the p. 91 page image by claude-fable-5-1 2026-09-14 — not yet human-read" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-09-14"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """SCOPE. The author's own constructivist-sense definition, offered after Gaines (Def. 8, "[that which] is distinguished as a system") and before Abbot & van Ness (Def. 10). Sorts: region, space; constraint: the boundary functionally and uniquely distinguishes the region. The paragraph before it fixes the sense of "functionally": "the act of drawing a distinction must necessarily be made with reference to and in the context of the system which makes or draws the distinction, usually a human subject. What that system chooses as significant can be called the function of the distinction." That is the observer's act stated inside the definition; a stance assertion (constitutive, in Gaines's and Klir's sense) is a candidate for the guided pass, not asserted here.

CASES. None ruled at this locus; the lever recurs as an illustration of the functional distinction ("all those entities which can act as levers") but is not offered as a system₂.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED. No cco is-about."""@en .
